WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:01.170
STEPHANIE WONG: Hey, everyone.

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:02.790
We've got a special
episode of "Take5"

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:05.250
today, where we're going to
answer the top 10 questions we

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:06.180
hear from customers.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.346
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Wouldn't
that make it a "Take10?"

00:00:09.346 --> 00:00:11.234
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.119 --> 00:00:15.910
STEPHANIE WONG: And
our first question is--

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:17.777
what exactly is a
customer engineer?

00:00:17.777 --> 00:00:19.110
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Great question.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:20.580
In a sentence,
customer engineers

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.660
exist to help our customers
transform, evolve,

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:28.200
and yes, engineer their business
applications on Google Cloud.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:29.700
We do that by meeting
with customers

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.009
one-on-one to understand
their current state

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:33.940
and where they'd like
to be in the future.

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.840
We then work with customers
to navigate the best path

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.030
to get them from
today to tomorrow,

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:41.920
help identify the right
technical solution within GCP,

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.010
assemble a proof of
concept, collaborate

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:45.810
with them with a
proper architecture,

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.510
and design a migration plan
to safely and securely migrate

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:50.250
their data to its destination.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.590
Our ultimate goal is to
help customers be successful

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:53.960
in Google Cloud platform.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:55.860
And we really own that
end-to-end technical

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:57.870
relationship between
Google Cloud and you.

00:00:57.870 --> 00:00:59.760
STEPHANIE WONG:
That's how we roll.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.820
Question number 2-- if I want
to run my application on GCP,

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:05.442
how do I choose
which product to use?

00:01:05.442 --> 00:01:06.150
NICK ORLOVE: Hmm.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:08.880
That's a tough one because,
honestly, it depends.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.740
I know that seems
like a cop-out answer.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:12.150
But to recommend
where you should

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.010
be running your
application, it's

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.980
best to fully understand your
application requirements.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:20.730
GCP offers four primary means
of application hosting--

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:23.910
those being Compute Engine,
Kubernetes Engine, App Engine,

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:25.380
and Cloud Functions.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:28.150
Google Compute Engine,
or GCE for short,

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:30.360
is our infrastructure as
a service offering, which

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.190
allows you to
create fully managed

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:35.070
and customizable VMs
for running your apps.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:37.920
If you're interested in running
a containerized application,

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:40.350
Google Kubernetes
Engine, or GKE,

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.510
might be the best
option, because GKE

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:45.010
Is our fully managed and hard
in container orchestration

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:46.370
and management service.

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:49.350
If managing the
infrastructure isn't your jam,

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:52.170
check out App Engine, which is
Google's platform as a service

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:54.300
solution, and lets
developers completely

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.310
focus on building
great applications

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:58.860
without the management overhead
of dealing with servers

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:00.930
or configuration deployments.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:03.200
Finally, Cloud
Functions is Google's

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.550
event-driven and
serverless platform

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:07.440
that only runs your
code in response

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:08.970
to event-driven triggers.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:12.120
The best news is you can also
pick a combination of computing

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:12.960
offerings.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.279
So you don't have
to just rely on one.

00:02:15.279 --> 00:02:16.570
YURI GRINSHTEYN: And I got one.

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:19.020
Now that we know about
application hosting,

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:20.790
my app obviously needs storage.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:23.686
Question 3-- how do I decide
where to store my data.

00:02:23.686 --> 00:02:25.435
STEPHANIE WONG: Well,
there are many fully

00:02:25.435 --> 00:02:27.720
managed offerings here
for that data that's

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:31.370
either structured, unstructured,
transactional, or relational.

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:33.150
Persistent Disk
offers block storage

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:35.730
that's suitable for VMs
and any kind of containers,

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:37.860
like Google Compute Engine
or Kubernetes Engine,

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:38.910
as you mentioned.

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:40.590
Cloud storage is used
for object storage

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.390
and can store
streaming multimedia,

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:45.030
or custom data analytics
pipelines, or even backups,

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:46.530
and disaster recovery.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.560
And then Cloud Bigtable is
a NoSQL wide-column database

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.440
that's suitable for real
time access and any kind

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.470
of analytics workloads that need
low latency and high throughput

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:56.940
read, write access.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:59.100
Cloud Data Store is a
NoSQL document database

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.410
for Web and mobile applications.

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:05.910
And Cloud SQL is a manage MySQL
and Postgres database service.

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:08.350
Cloud Spanner is a mission
critical relational database

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:11.790
service with transactional
consistency and global scale.

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:14.220
And cloud BigQuery is a
fully managed Enterprise Data

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.290
warehouse with SQL
syntax and real time

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:17.830
response for ad hoc querying.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:20.070
Lastly, we have a number
of databases and storage

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:23.780
used for Firebase, our mobile
and web app hosting platform.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:25.996
NICK ORLOVE: Oh, hey,
a penny on the floor.

00:03:25.996 --> 00:03:28.440
[WHOOSH]

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:30.420
IVAN RAMIREZ: I think
we took a wrong turn.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.850
Oh well, onward with
question number 4.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.380
Everyone is talking about
machine learning these days.

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:38.410
But I just don't know where
and how to get started.

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:41.220
What's the best way to get
started with ML if not a data

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:42.990
scientist or robot?

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.690
CORI PEELE: Well, Google wants
to democratize AI and make

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:49.650
it accessible and useful for
enterprises and developers.

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:52.010
We offer ready-to-use
models so you

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:55.110
can focus on the application
and not the science.

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:59.250
Using our machine learning
REST APIs is quite simple.

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:02.280
Google Cloud provides
fully trained machine

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:06.480
learning models for vision,
video intelligence, speech,

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.580
translation, natural
language processing.

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:12.540
And these are extremely
easy to use if you are

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:14.940
a developer familiar with REST.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:18.570
So the ML APIs are
pre-trained on Google datasets

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:20.550
and ready to go.

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:23.760
Anyone that knows how
to call a REST API

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.100
can integrate with them
from their applications.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.770
If you want to train
your own datasets,

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:30.900
instead of relying
on Google's data,

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.510
then Cloud AutoML
allows you to create

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:35.670
models specific to your domain.

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:38.430
No programming experience or
machine learning experience

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:39.630
is required.

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:43.860
You simply identify your
training data and labels,

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:46.440
and Cloud AutoML does the rest.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.740
Lest we have the
world's most popular ML

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:52.110
framework, TensorFlow--

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:54.720
and this is available
as a managed service

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:56.070
called Cloud ML.

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:58.770
This is for those with
theoretical machine learning

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:02.650
knowledge and those who are
comfortable writing code.

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:04.950
Check out the blog
post, "Learn TensorFlow

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:07.800
and deep learning
without a PhD" if you're

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.680
a developer interested
in this option.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:11.520
IVAN RAMIREZ: It sounds like
Google has a lot of experience

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:13.170
with big data and
machine learning.

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:16.350
Question 5-- if my
business is running on GCP,

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:19.682
does Google analyze the data
I've stored in the Cloud?

00:05:19.682 --> 00:05:20.890
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Not at all.

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:22.200
Many people are concerned
about their data.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:23.741
And privacy is a
big deal for Google,

00:05:23.741 --> 00:05:25.320
as well as every other company.

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:29.080
Our focus is on making sure only
you have access to your data.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:31.510
Not only does Google not
read or analyze your data,

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:33.636
we also encrypt everything
and give you the ability

00:05:33.636 --> 00:05:35.760
to use your own encryption
keys to make sure you're

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:37.050
the only one who can read it.

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:39.092
This applies to all of
the Google Cloud Services.

00:05:39.092 --> 00:05:40.883
IVAN RAMIREZ: Thanks
everyone for watching.

00:05:40.883 --> 00:05:43.800
Join us in the next episode with
the remaining top 5 questions.

